  <content>
  <section><title>Solving Equations and Creating Equivalent Equations</title>
<para>One way to create equivalent equations is to solve the equation. In each step of solving, an equivalent equation is created.</para>
<para>Here is a general strategy for solving linear equations:</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Simplify each side of the equation as much as possible.</para>
<para>Use the Distributive Property to remove any parentheses.
  Combine like terms.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Collect all the variable terms on one side of the equation.</para>
<para>Use the Addition or Subtraction Property of Equality.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis> Collect all the constant terms on the other side of the equation.</para>
<para>Use the Addition or Subtraction Property of Equality.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis>Make the coefficient of the variable term equal to 1.</para>
<para>Use the Multiplication or Division Property of Equality.
  State the solution to the equation.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5 - </emphasis>Check the solution.</para>
<para>Substitute the solution into the original equation to make sure the result is a true statement.</para>

<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 1</para>
<para>Here is an example of solving equations. Notice that each step is an acceptable move that creates an equivalent equation.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Simplify each side of the equation as much as possible.</para>
<para>Use the Distributive Property to remove any parentheses.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}-6(x+3)&amp;=&amp;24 \\ -6x-18&amp;=&amp;24 \end{array}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Collect all the variable terms on one side of the equation.</para>
<para>Nothing to do. All the variables of \(x\) are on the left side. </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis> Collect all the constant terms on the other side of the equation.</para>
<para>Use the Addition or Subtraction Property of Equality.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}-6x-18 {\style{color:red}+} {\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}8}&amp;=&amp;24 {\style{color:red}+} {\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}8} \\ -6x &amp;=&amp;42\end{array}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis>Make the coefficient of the variable term equal to 1.</para>
<para>Use the Multiplication or Division Property of Equality.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}\frac{-6x}{6}&amp;=&amp;\frac{42}{-6} \\ x&amp;=&amp;7 \end{array}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5 - </emphasis>Check the solution.</para>
<para>Substitute the solution into the original equation to make sure the result is a true statement.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}-6(x+3)&amp;=&amp;24 \\ -6({\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}7}+3)&amp;=&amp;24 \\ -6(-4)&amp;=&amp;24 \\24&amp;=&amp;24 \checkmark \end{array}\)</para>

<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 2</para>
<para>Find the solution for \(3(4x - 1) - 2 = 8x + 3\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Simplify each side of the equation as much as possible.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}3(4x - 1) - 2&amp;=&amp;8x + 3 \\ 12x - 3 - 2&amp;=&amp;8x + 3 \\ 12x - 5&amp;=&amp;8x + 3 \\\end{array}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Collect all the variable terms on one side of the equation.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}12x - 5 - 8x&amp;=&amp;8x + 3 - 8x \\ 4x - 5 &amp;=&amp; 3\\\end{array}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Collect all the constant terms on the other side of the equation.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}4x - 5 + 5 &amp;=&amp; 3 + 5 \\ 4x &amp;=&amp; 8\end{array}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Make the coefficient of the variable term equal to 1.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}\frac{4x}{4} &amp;=&amp; \frac84 \\ x &amp;=&amp; 2\end{array}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> Check the solution.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}3(4x - 1) - 2&amp;=&amp;8x + 3 ? \\ 3(4(2) - 1) - 2&amp;=&amp;8(2) + 3 ? \\ 3(8 - 1) - 2&amp;=&amp;16 + 3 ? \\ 3(7) - 2&amp;=&amp;19 ? \\ 21 - 2&amp;=&amp;19 ? \\ 19&amp;=&amp;19 \checkmark\end{array}\)</para>

<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 3</para>
<para>Solve \(12x + 3(x + 7) = 10x - 24\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Simplify each side of the equation as much as possible.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}12x + 3(x + 7) = 10x - 24 \\ 12x + 3x + 21 = 10x - 24 \\ 15x + 21 = 10x - 24 \end{array}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Collect all the variable terms on one side of the equation.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}15x + 21 -10x&amp;=&amp;10x-24-10x \\ 5x + 21&amp;=&amp;- 24\end{array}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Collect all the constant terms on the other side of the equation.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}5x+21-21&amp;=&amp;-24-21 \\ 5x&amp;=&amp;-45\end{array}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Make the coefficient of the variable term equal to 1.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}\frac{5x}{5}&amp;=&amp;-\frac{45}{5} \\ x &amp;=&amp; -9\end{array}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> Check the solution.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}12x + 3(x + 7)&amp;=&amp;10x - 24 ? \\ 12(-9) + 3(-9 + 7)&amp;=&amp;10(-9) - 24 ? \\ -108 + 3(-2)&amp;=&amp;-90 - 24 ? \\ -108 + -6&amp;=&amp;-86 ? \\ -114&amp;=&amp;-114 \checkmark\end{array}\)</para>

